WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.065
Continuo, part three.

2
00:00:01.335 --> 00:00:02.730
Finding the notes on the board.

3
00:00:04.757 --> 00:00:06.590
Six streams of numbers, one per string,

4
00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:08.466
arrive at one chip, on an AMD Z C U one oh

5
00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:08.882
two board.

6
00:00:09.132 --> 00:00:11.049
Here it is on the bench: the green board

7
00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:11.633
in the centre.

8
00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:13.758
The small board on the left, with the blue

9
00:00:13.758 --> 00:00:15.633
trimmers, is Sena's, from the last video.

10
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:17.800
The chip is two things in one package: a

11
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.384
field of logic that the team wires up

12
00:00:19.384 --> 00:00:21.093
itself, and a small ordinary processor

13
00:00:21.093 --> 00:00:21.509
beside it.

14
00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:23.676
The logic does the work on every sample;

15
00:00:23.676 --> 00:00:25.427
the processor does the work on every note.

16
00:00:28.997 --> 00:00:31.036
The chip does two jobs for every string,

17
00:00:31.036 --> 00:00:33.123
side by side, and both listen to the same

18
00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:33.455
string.

19
00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.064
The first asks when: is there a note, and

20
00:00:36.064 --> 00:00:36.490
how hard?

21
00:00:36.775 --> 00:00:38.150
That is Dini's note detector.

22
00:00:38.435 --> 00:00:40.522
The second asks what: which note is it?

23
00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:42.087
That is Felix's pitch core.

24
00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:44.364
They are built differently, because the

25
00:00:44.364 --> 00:00:45.502
questions are different.

26
00:00:45.786 --> 00:00:47.589
A start can be spotted almost at once.

27
00:00:47.873 --> 00:00:49.960
Naming the note takes a little longer, as

28
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:50.529
we will see.

29
00:00:50.814 --> 00:00:52.948
Six strings, so six of each, all working

30
00:00:52.948 --> 00:00:53.754
at the same time.

31
00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:56.126
The small processor on the same chip, the

32
00:00:56.126 --> 00:00:58.165
ARM, joins the answers into one note for

33
00:00:58.165 --> 00:00:58.687
the laptop.

34
00:00:58.972 --> 00:01:00.916
Every block was built in Python first,

35
00:01:00.916 --> 00:01:03.003
then the hardware was checked against it,

36
00:01:03.003 --> 00:01:03.572
bit for bit.

37
00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:09.508
Dini's detector watches one thing: the

38
00:01:09.508 --> 00:01:11.600
attack energy of the string, moment to

39
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:11.957
moment.

40
00:01:12.263 --> 00:01:13.793
The trick is what it measures.

41
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.344
Not the loudness itself, but how fast the

42
00:01:16.344 --> 00:01:18.487
sound changes from one number to the next.

43
00:01:18.793 --> 00:01:21.191
A pick striking a string changes it fast;

44
00:01:21.191 --> 00:01:22.976
a string ringing on changes slowly.

45
00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:25.476
So the pluck stands out, and the ringing

46
00:01:25.476 --> 00:01:25.935
does not.

47
00:01:26.241 --> 00:01:27.109
Here is one note.

48
00:01:27.415 --> 00:01:29.660
Think of a real D three, as on the poster.

49
00:01:29.966 --> 00:01:31.700
A jump in the energy is the start.

50
00:01:32.006 --> 00:01:34.098
The loudest point of the sound, in the

51
00:01:34.098 --> 00:01:36.292
first forty milliseconds or so, says how

52
00:01:36.292 --> 00:01:37.669
hard the string was played.

53
00:01:37.975 --> 00:01:40.118
And when the energy fades below a line,

54
00:01:40.118 --> 00:01:41.087
the note has ended.

55
00:01:43.021 --> 00:01:45.047
Six of these run at once, one for each

56
00:01:45.047 --> 00:01:47.123
string, and the settings can be changed

57
00:01:47.123 --> 00:01:49.099
while it runs, without rebuilding the

58
00:01:49.099 --> 00:01:49.346
chip.

59
00:01:49.643 --> 00:01:51.421
On the processor, two more checks.

60
00:01:51.718 --> 00:01:53.793
A second start a blink after the first is

61
00:01:53.793 --> 00:01:55.127
a bounce, and is dropped.

62
00:01:55.424 --> 00:01:57.499
And a start with nothing ringing after it

63
00:01:57.499 --> 00:01:59.525
was a finger on the string, not a note.

64
00:02:03.213 --> 00:02:05.384
Felix's pitch core asks which note it is.

65
00:02:05.702 --> 00:02:08.032
It takes the string's wave, and a copy of

66
00:02:08.032 --> 00:02:09.568
it, shifted along a little.

67
00:02:09.885 --> 00:02:12.162
Slide the copy, and measure the mismatch

68
00:02:12.162 --> 00:02:12.956
at every shift.

69
00:02:13.274 --> 00:02:15.551
At one shift the copy sits right on top of

70
00:02:15.551 --> 00:02:18.146
the wave again: that shift is one period,

71
00:02:18.146 --> 00:02:19.893
and the mismatch dips to nothing.

72
00:02:20.211 --> 00:02:21.429
The period is the note.

73
00:02:21.747 --> 00:02:23.653
Why not read the frequency directly?

74
00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.194
A frequency analysis needs far more sound

75
00:02:26.194 --> 00:02:28.154
to tell neighbouring low notes apart.

76
00:02:28.471 --> 00:02:29.954
This needs only a few waves.

77
00:02:30.272 --> 00:02:32.496
And each core searches only the notes its

78
00:02:32.496 --> 00:02:34.826
own string can play, which keeps it small.

79
00:02:36.885 --> 00:02:38.638
A low string's wave is longer, so the core

80
00:02:38.638 --> 00:02:40.274
has to hear more of it before it can name

81
00:02:40.274 --> 00:02:40.624
the note.

82
00:02:40.858 --> 00:02:42.455
It needs about forty-one milliseconds of

83
00:02:42.455 --> 00:02:44.130
sound on the low E, and about ten on the

84
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:44.403
high E.

85
00:02:44.636 --> 00:02:46.350
Those are design values: how the cores

86
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:46.779
were sized.

87
00:02:47.012 --> 00:02:48.531
That is physics, not computing power.

88
00:02:48.765 --> 00:02:50.440
A core cannot name a wave it has not heard

89
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:50.907
a few times.

90
00:02:51.141 --> 00:02:52.738
And each core gives a fresh answer about

91
00:02:52.738 --> 00:02:54.413
every five milliseconds, all the time,

92
00:02:54.413 --> 00:02:55.932
whether or not a note has just started.

93
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:00.975
Now the pieces come together.

94
00:03:01.272 --> 00:03:03.646
From Dini's detector: the start, and how

95
00:03:03.646 --> 00:03:03.894
hard.

96
00:03:04.191 --> 00:03:05.922
From Felix's core: which note.

97
00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:08.297
And from the detector again: the end.

98
00:03:08.594 --> 00:03:10.227
The ARM joins them into one note.

99
00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.700
The start is known first; the pitch is

100
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.075
attached when the core is confident of it;

101
00:03:15.075 --> 00:03:17.103
the end comes when the string goes quiet.

102
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.627
Each piece goes down the cable in turn:

103
00:03:19.627 --> 00:03:21.853
three small messages per note, one for the

104
00:03:21.853 --> 00:03:24.129
start, one for the pitch, one for the end.

105
00:03:24.426 --> 00:03:26.553
All three carry the same note number, so

106
00:03:26.553 --> 00:03:28.433
the laptop knows they belong together.

107
00:03:28.857 --> 00:03:30.719
By design, the note's start leaves the

108
00:03:30.719 --> 00:03:32.581
chip about forty-five milliseconds after

109
00:03:32.581 --> 00:03:33.035
the pluck.

110
00:03:33.308 --> 00:03:35.306
That is a design value, not a measurement.

111
00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:36.941
Most of that is not detection.

112
00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:39.030
It is waiting to hear how hard the note

113
00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:40.847
was played, because the loudest point

114
00:03:40.847 --> 00:03:42.754
comes a little after the start, and the

115
00:03:42.754 --> 00:03:43.708
message carries both.

116
00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.842
The pitch usually follows, and the end

117
00:03:45.842 --> 00:03:46.705
when the note ends.

118
00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.809
So the board hears each string on its own,

119
00:03:50.809 --> 00:03:53.175
finds each note as it starts, how hard,

120
00:03:53.175 --> 00:03:55.540
which note and when it ends, and sends it

121
00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.992
on as three small messages.

122
00:03:57.315 --> 00:03:59.842
Dini's detectors, Felix's pitch cores, and

123
00:03:59.842 --> 00:04:01.347
the join, all on one chip.

124
00:04:01.669 --> 00:04:03.497
Next: the band on the laptop.

